            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Spiritual exposure, shame, guilt, and moral vulnerability resulting from sin, as well as the absence of righteous covering.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 3:7\",\"Genesis 3:10-11\",\"Revelation 3:17-18\",\"2 Corinthians 5:3\",\"Exodus 32:25\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Original innocence, purity, and unhindered fellowship with God prior to the Fall.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 2:25\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Divine judgment, public humiliation, and the stripping away of worldly power, wealth, or divine protection.\",\"basis\":[\"Isaiah 20:2-4\",\"Micah 1:8\",\"Ezekiel 16:39\",\"Revelation 17:16\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Absolute transparency, truth, and human accountability before God's omniscience, demonstrating that nothing can be hidden.\",\"basis\":[\"Hebrews 4:13\",\"Job 1:21\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic meaning of nakedness in biblical theology is firmly anchored in the Eden narrative. It shifts from symbolizing unashamed purity and innocence before the Fall to representing profound guilt, exposure, and the need for divine covering (often linked to atonement) after the Fall. In prophetic literature, nakedness is frequently employed as a metaphor for the humiliation and vulnerability of judged nations.\"}",
